(Pathophysiology and Health Impacts of Airborne Pollutants) Tj T*

(Document ID: REF-HLT-2024) Tj T*
(Source: Global Pulmonary & Environmental Medicine Journal) Tj T*
(Topic: Physiological Effects of PM2.5, PM10, NO2, O3, and SO2) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Pollutant Pathophysiology:) Tj T*
(1. PM2.5 \(Fine Particles\): Inhaled into pulmonary alveoli; translocates to bloodstream; triggers systemic oxidation, arterial stiffness, and myocardial strain.) Tj T*
(2. PM10 \(Coarse Particles\): Deposited in upper airways; causes throat irritation, coughing, and bronchitis exacerbation.) Tj T*
(3. Nitrogen Dioxide \(NO2\): Causes bronchial airway inflammation, asthma exacerbation, and reduced lung capacity.) Tj T*
(4. Ground-Level Ozone \(O3\): Powerful oxidant that damages lung lining tissue, causing chest tightness and deep breathing discomfort.) Tj T*
(5. Sulfur Dioxide \(SO2\): Induces bronchoconstriction in asthmatic individuals within minutes of exposure.) Tj T*
